  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 255 PM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Can't rule out an isolated shower or storm in the \nNW Piedmont late this afternoon and early this evening.\n\nSubtropical ridging extends from TX into the mid MS Valley as a \nmid/upper low is centered over the eastern Gulf. Scattered \nconvection is beginning to develop over the western NC mountains, \nwhile an area of decaying showers associated with a ripple in the \nflow aloft is located over southern WV and northwestern VA. Many \nhigh-res models have some more isolated showers and storms \ndeveloping over SW VA and NW NC later this afternoon, perhaps being \nenhanced by the previously mentioned upper disturbance. Given SBCAPE \nof 1000-2000 J/kg and a tongue of relatively higher PW values around \n1.7\" across the NW Piedmont, a few of these showers and storms may \nmake it as far as our NW Piedmont before diminishing with loss of \ndaytime heating. The best chance will be from about 6-10 PM. No \norganized severe weather is expected, but can't rule out isolated \ngusty winds given marginal DCAPE of 600-800 J/kg.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... An approaching cold front will bring the potential \nfor strong to severe thunderstorms on Monday, followed by a chance \nfor heavy rainfall on Tuesday.\n\nAs a cold front slowly moves south across the Appalachians, \nMonday should be the last day with widespread upper 80 and 90 degree \ntemperatures across central North Carolina. Although Monday morning \nwill start off sunny, as the front begins to drop to the south, \ncloud cover will increase and showers/storms will begin to fire \nearly in the afternoon along the VA/NC border. The greatest chance \nfor rain will be along the state border, although most locations \nwill have at least some chance for rain, particularly in the \nevening. The wind shear that could provide better organization for \nthunderstorms should remain to the north across Virginia, although \nMUCAPE locally will range from 1000 to 2000 J/kg. There will still \nbe the potential for some isolated strong to severe thunderstorms \nacross central North Carolina, particularly to the west of US-1. The \nmost likely timing for severe thunderstorms should be between 2-6pm \nwith the threat coming from damaging winds. In addition, cannot rule \nout the potential for some flash flooding near and around the Triad \nwith the urban environment allowing for a better chance of excessive \nrainfall occurring.\n\nWith the front moving south into North Carolina Monday night into \nTuesday, that will put the forecast area onto the cool side of the \nboundary, which should reduce/eliminate the chance for severe \nweather on Tuesday. However, widespread rainfall is expected, and \nalong the cool side of the boundary, high rainfall rates will still \nbe possible, with the entire area will be under a marginal (level 1 \nof 4) risk for excessive rainfall, primarily Tuesday afternoon.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Cold-air damming will develop by the middle of the \nweek, bringing several days of well below normal temperatures.\n\nWith the front slipping to the south of the area late Tuesday, \na ridge of high pressure will extend south into the Carolinas from a \nstrong high that will remain over Quebec for several days. While \nmodels are coming into better agreement that a low pressure system \nwill develop off the Atlantic coast Thursday and move north, there \nis good model agreement that a sustained period of northeast wind \nand widespread cloud cover will occur Wednesday and Thursday. \nWednesday will be the first full day of fall, and it will feel like \nit - locations north and west of I-85 are unlikely to rise out of \nthe 60s both Wednesday and Thursday, with the rest of the area only \nrising into the 70s. Widespread light rain is likely on Wednesday, \nwith conditions beginning to dry out on Thursday. The pattern, both \nwith temperature and precipitation, should begin to moderate for \nFriday through Sunday, primarily based on model predictions of the \nAtlantic low moving towards New England and pushing the ridge of \nhigh pressure to the west. The end of this week looks to favor \nmostly sunny skies, minimal chances for rainfall, and highs in the \n70s. Roxboro could see its temperature dip into the 40s on Saturday \nmorning for the first time in several months.",
